WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.090 --> 00:00:01.810
- Nobody saw those movies, right?

00:00:01.810 --> 00:00:03.157
Because nobody says,

00:00:03.157 --> 00:00:05.347
"Honey, let's look through
the TV Guide thing,

00:00:05.347 --> 00:00:07.550
"and see if there's any wonderful things

00:00:07.550 --> 00:00:08.467
about racism,
(Dave laughs)

00:00:08.467 --> 00:00:10.160
"or any things about diversity."

00:00:10.160 --> 00:00:11.220
I really...
- Yeah.

00:00:11.220 --> 00:00:12.963
You know what I'd like
at the end of the day?

00:00:12.963 --> 00:00:17.370
Is a really great diversity humor, right?

00:00:17.370 --> 00:00:18.460
There isn't any.

00:00:18.460 --> 00:00:21.127
(bright music)

00:00:32.760 --> 00:00:34.310
- I'm Dave Rubin, and joining me today,

00:00:34.310 --> 00:00:35.800
is an American playwright,

00:00:35.800 --> 00:00:39.507
a filmmaker whose works
included "Glengarry Glen Ross,

00:00:39.507 --> 00:00:42.270
"The Verdict, Wag the Dog," and more.

00:00:42.270 --> 00:00:45.127
And author of the new book, "Recessional:

00:00:45.127 --> 00:00:48.960
"The Death of Free Speech and
the Cost of a Free Lunch,"

00:00:48.960 --> 00:00:51.110
I've got a copy in my hand right now,

00:00:51.110 --> 00:00:53.570
David Mamet, welcome to the Rubin Report.

00:00:53.570 --> 00:00:55.350
- Thanks so much, glad to be here.

00:00:55.350 --> 00:00:58.340
- I've wanted to talk to
you for quite some time.

00:00:58.340 --> 00:01:00.830
And I was going through
the book this morning,

00:01:00.830 --> 00:01:02.630
and I thought, man,

00:01:02.630 --> 00:01:05.960
this guy sees the world
sort of as I see the world.

00:01:05.960 --> 00:01:08.360
So why don't we do a little 101 first,

00:01:08.360 --> 00:01:11.103
for people that aren't
that familiar with ya.

00:01:12.120 --> 00:01:15.970
What got you into playwriting,
writing screenplays?

00:01:15.970 --> 00:01:17.670
Did you always have something to say

00:01:17.670 --> 00:01:19.683
and wanted to put the pen to paper?

00:01:21.000 --> 00:01:23.230
- Well, yeah, I was raised as a smartass,

00:01:23.230 --> 00:01:25.067
and everyone always said when I was a kid,

00:01:25.067 --> 00:01:26.370
"Nobody likes a smartass,"

00:01:26.370 --> 00:01:28.520
but I didn't find that to be true

00:01:28.520 --> 00:01:32.670
because I always made jokes,
and I always dramatized.

00:01:32.670 --> 00:01:34.047
My mother used to say, (chuckles)

00:01:34.047 --> 00:01:36.120
"David, why must you
dramatize everything?"

00:01:36.120 --> 00:01:37.930
But because that's what I, you know,

00:01:37.930 --> 00:01:39.510
I was born with that gene,

00:01:39.510 --> 00:01:43.200
just like Sandy Koufax was
born to throw the fastball.

00:01:43.200 --> 00:01:46.440
And because that worked,
I never did anything else.

00:01:46.440 --> 00:01:48.340
I would never do any school work.

00:01:48.340 --> 00:01:51.720
And I worked at a million menial jobs.

00:01:51.720 --> 00:01:53.550
We'd call them entry level jobs now,

00:01:53.550 --> 00:01:55.430
we called them menial jobs then.

00:01:55.430 --> 00:01:59.230
But when I realized there was
this thing called the theater,

00:01:59.230 --> 00:02:01.970
and that I could actually get in there

00:02:01.970 --> 00:02:06.630
and have the most fun in the
world, I was never coming out.

00:02:06.630 --> 00:02:09.110
And that's the thing that
I see about young people,

00:02:09.110 --> 00:02:11.880
whatever they're going into,
if they discover something,

00:02:11.880 --> 00:02:14.340
for example, aviation, or
they discover the theater,

00:02:14.340 --> 00:02:15.773
they discover the military.

00:02:17.610 --> 00:02:19.383
If it's in their DNA,

00:02:20.410 --> 00:02:22.467
they say, "You can kill me,

00:02:22.467 --> 00:02:24.920
"but you're gonna have to kill
me 'cause I'm never leaving."

00:02:24.920 --> 00:02:27.270
So that's how I was about theater.

00:02:27.270 --> 00:02:29.720
So I started writing plays
when I was very young,

00:02:29.720 --> 00:02:33.112
and got from there into
writing and directing movies.

00:02:33.112 --> 00:02:35.960
And the rest is, you know, there it is.

00:02:35.960 --> 00:02:38.530
- Yeah, so I do wanna jump
into the movies a little bit,

00:02:38.530 --> 00:02:40.960
but let's start with the book because

00:02:40.960 --> 00:02:44.810
your defense of free speech,
but really beyond free speech,

00:02:44.810 --> 00:02:47.510
your defense of America
and your explanation

00:02:47.510 --> 00:02:50.840
of sort of this very strange
pivotal moment that we're in,

00:02:50.840 --> 00:02:53.590
where we're either about to continue,

00:02:53.590 --> 00:02:55.550
or maybe reinvent it perhaps,

00:02:55.550 --> 00:02:59.950
or just let the whole thing
go, I think is really apropos.

00:02:59.950 --> 00:03:01.380
It's what I'm thinking about a lot.

00:03:01.380 --> 00:03:04.410
It's what I'm talking about
on my show all the time.

00:03:04.410 --> 00:03:06.150
When did you really start realizing

00:03:06.150 --> 00:03:07.593
something was wrong here?

00:03:09.720 --> 00:03:11.170
- Well, I wrote a book about 20 years ago,

00:03:11.170 --> 00:03:15.700
called, "The Secret
Knowledge," a political book.

00:03:15.700 --> 00:03:18.870
And it was looking at
what happened in New York,

00:03:18.870 --> 00:03:21.760
and was happening in the theater,
and what was happening...

00:03:24.750 --> 00:03:26.720
I grew up as a red diaper baby, right?

00:03:26.720 --> 00:03:28.540
My dad was a labor lawyer.

00:03:28.540 --> 00:03:30.710
And everybody we knew,

00:03:30.710 --> 00:03:32.680
the more radical people
were the communists,

00:03:32.680 --> 00:03:37.060
but in our thing, they were all
first generation immigrants.

00:03:37.060 --> 00:03:39.430
They just wanted to make a
living and they were Liberals.

00:03:39.430 --> 00:03:41.660
And what Liberal meant then,

00:03:41.660 --> 00:03:45.500
was give some money to the NAACP, NAACP,

00:03:45.500 --> 00:03:50.133
and put your kid in a
Episcopal reformed temple.

00:03:51.721 --> 00:03:54.660
And they'd all fought
in the Second World War.

00:03:54.660 --> 00:03:56.650
So everybody loved America,

00:03:56.650 --> 00:04:00.560
but America to them meant do
your job, raise your kids,

00:04:00.560 --> 00:04:02.530
don't get in the other guy's way,

00:04:02.530 --> 00:04:04.760
exhibit a little tolerance if you can,

00:04:04.760 --> 00:04:07.940
and try to take care of yourself.

00:04:07.940 --> 00:04:10.040
Although at that time, and again, now,

00:04:10.040 --> 00:04:11.830
we Jews were a despised race.

00:04:11.830 --> 00:04:16.170
When I was a kid, there was
still restricted hotels,

00:04:16.170 --> 00:04:18.440
didn't accept Jews, clubs that didn't.

00:04:18.440 --> 00:04:22.190
They were very open about
it, no Hebrews need apply.

00:04:22.190 --> 00:04:24.630
Professions that didn't accept Jews.

00:04:24.630 --> 00:04:27.700
And so, like as Jews down
for the last 2,000 years,

00:04:27.700 --> 00:04:31.427
we said, "Well, okay,
I can figure this out.

00:04:31.427 --> 00:04:34.337
"I'll keep my head down,
and I hope nobody kills me."

00:04:35.240 --> 00:04:36.860
And so I was born right after,

00:04:36.860 --> 00:04:38.630
two years after they
were throwing my people,

00:04:38.630 --> 00:04:40.370
our people into ovens.

00:04:40.370 --> 00:04:43.773
So that's the world that we
grew up in, that I grew up in.

00:04:46.895 --> 00:04:49.640
And I discovered, first of
all, my Jewish identity.

00:04:49.640 --> 00:04:52.447
And I said, "Wait a
second, I'm at Jew," right?

00:04:52.447 --> 00:04:55.750
"Was a great people, we don't
have to keep our head down."

00:04:55.750 --> 00:04:58.210
I wanted discover what
it means to be a Jew.

00:04:58.210 --> 00:05:01.447
So I started studying
the Torah and I said,

00:05:01.447 --> 00:05:02.437
"Okay, there's two things.

00:05:02.437 --> 00:05:04.380
"One, there is Yiddishkeit," right?

00:05:04.380 --> 00:05:09.380
The idea of the Eastern
Jewish, the Ashkenazi heritage,

00:05:09.550 --> 00:05:11.670
where all my people came from,

00:05:11.670 --> 00:05:14.960
you know, which was Broadway
and Upper Broadway in New York,

00:05:14.960 --> 00:05:17.560
and gefilte fish, and speaking
a little bit of Yiddish,

00:05:17.560 --> 00:05:19.270
and blah, blah, blah, very good.

00:05:19.270 --> 00:05:21.480
But the Nazis, the
Germans destroyed there,

00:05:21.480 --> 00:05:23.070
it doesn't exist anymore.

00:05:23.070 --> 00:05:25.120
It still existed when I was a kid

00:05:25.120 --> 00:05:28.310
because there were the immigrant
generation and their kids,

00:05:28.310 --> 00:05:29.483
it was my parents,

00:05:30.800 --> 00:05:33.030
and they're gone now.

00:05:33.030 --> 00:05:35.370
And so that civilization is just,

00:05:35.370 --> 00:05:38.980
the magnificent 1,000-year-long
very rich civilization

00:05:38.980 --> 00:05:40.853
of the Eastern European Jews is gone.

00:05:42.730 --> 00:05:45.220
But the thing which is
not gone is the Torah.

00:05:45.220 --> 00:05:48.420
And so I started studying the
Torah, and reading the Torah.

00:05:48.420 --> 00:05:49.777
And I said, "Oh my god,

00:05:49.777 --> 00:05:53.907
"this is a record of human experience.

00:05:53.907 --> 00:05:56.410
"It's told in story form."

00:05:56.410 --> 00:05:59.220
But we Jews tell everything
in a story form, right?

00:05:59.220 --> 00:06:01.527
That's how we communicate
with each other, right?

00:06:01.527 --> 00:06:03.223
And I realized, I said,

00:06:04.647 --> 00:06:07.037
"They said that the German humor

00:06:07.037 --> 00:06:09.137
"is all based on the bathroom,

00:06:09.137 --> 00:06:12.447
"and French humor is all
based on the bedroom,

00:06:12.447 --> 00:06:14.970
"but Jewish humor is
all based on the mind."

00:06:14.970 --> 00:06:18.680
It's based on ambiguity, incredible,

00:06:18.680 --> 00:06:22.453
inbred adoration of ambiguity.

00:06:25.004 --> 00:06:29.510
And the Torah takes it
down to the bare bones.

00:06:29.510 --> 00:06:30.343
And it says, "Wait a second,

00:06:30.343 --> 00:06:33.017
"here's something you aren't
gonna understand," right?

00:06:33.017 --> 00:06:35.633
"Why did God create the snake?

00:06:36.900 --> 00:06:41.687
Why did God, the first act
that human beings performed,

00:06:42.770 --> 00:06:44.120
first off, Eve

00:06:45.860 --> 00:06:47.240
turns her back on God.

00:06:47.240 --> 00:06:50.490
And then, the first
act that Adam performs,

00:06:50.490 --> 00:06:54.650
the first thing that a human man says is,

00:06:54.650 --> 00:06:56.960
he blames his wife, he rats her out.

00:06:56.960 --> 00:06:58.540
He says, "The woman made me do it."

00:06:58.540 --> 00:06:59.800
So if you read through the Torah,

00:06:59.800 --> 00:07:02.707
time and time again, you
say, "I just don't get this.

00:07:02.707 --> 00:07:04.270
"I wanna make sense of this."

00:07:04.270 --> 00:07:07.830
It's not how to do this and
you'll be a good person.

00:07:07.830 --> 00:07:10.100
It's things which are shocking.

00:07:10.100 --> 00:07:11.560
So when I grew up, everybody was,

00:07:11.560 --> 00:07:14.410
all the Jews were about
Buddhism and Zen Buddhism.

00:07:14.410 --> 00:07:16.710
And what the sound of
one hand is clapping,

00:07:16.710 --> 00:07:18.030
and the Zen koans.

00:07:18.030 --> 00:07:19.750
Well, okay, very good, indeed.

00:07:19.750 --> 00:07:23.900
But everything in the Torah
is a provocative koan.

00:07:23.900 --> 00:07:26.627
It just doesn't make sense
until you have to look at it,

00:07:26.627 --> 00:07:29.610
and look at it, and figure
out what did it mean?

00:07:29.610 --> 00:07:30.810
And when you figure it out,

00:07:30.810 --> 00:07:32.790
you realize it's all about human nature,

00:07:32.790 --> 00:07:34.960
which means about my nature,

00:07:34.960 --> 00:07:37.570
that everything in the
Torah is about betrayal,

00:07:37.570 --> 00:07:41.603
and incest, and adultery,
and bastardy, and murder.

00:07:42.650 --> 00:07:46.570
And that's who we are, that
we're incredibly flawed.

00:07:46.570 --> 00:07:51.570
So I took that study and that
understanding of the world,

00:07:51.820 --> 00:07:55.850
and to tried to apply it to
the world that I see around me.

00:07:55.850 --> 00:07:58.250
I said, "Wait a second,
how did we end up here?"

00:07:59.200 --> 00:08:04.200
So the answer is not, the
Democrats took us here, right?

00:08:04.400 --> 00:08:05.380
Which they did.

00:08:05.380 --> 00:08:09.480
Or that the Marxists have
come out of the woodwork.

00:08:09.480 --> 00:08:11.320
Or that the, which they have,

00:08:11.320 --> 00:08:13.203
or that the press has,

00:08:14.750 --> 00:08:16.803
has imploded upon itself.

00:08:18.520 --> 00:08:19.880
The answer is something different,

00:08:19.880 --> 00:08:23.690
that these things that occurred
to me are not the cost,

00:08:23.690 --> 00:08:25.280
but they're the result.

00:08:25.280 --> 00:08:27.360
So they're the result of what?

00:08:27.360 --> 00:08:31.150
They're the result of a
certain point in civilization.

00:08:31.150 --> 00:08:34.550
And so if you look at
the Torah, that point is,

00:08:34.550 --> 00:08:36.860
it's perfectly clear, the Tower of Babel.

00:08:36.860 --> 00:08:39.020
Because the richest people in the world,

00:08:39.020 --> 00:08:39.853
what do they wanna do?

00:08:39.853 --> 00:08:44.580
They wanted raise a tower to
Heaven so they can be like God.

00:08:44.580 --> 00:08:46.680
And it all came tumbling down around them.

00:08:46.680 --> 00:08:48.420
So I say, "Okay, I get it."

00:08:48.420 --> 00:08:49.873
What's Green Peace trying to do?

00:08:49.873 --> 00:08:53.380
They're trying to raise,
what's the ACLU trying to do?

00:08:53.380 --> 00:08:55.690
They're trying to raise a tower to God

00:08:55.690 --> 00:08:57.260
so they can be like God,

00:08:57.260 --> 00:08:59.400
so they can be in charge of the world.

00:08:59.400 --> 00:09:02.540
So I started to see the world
in a very, very different way.

00:09:02.540 --> 00:09:05.740
And so I said, "What
brought us to this point?"

00:09:05.740 --> 00:09:08.580
Well, the same thing that
brought the Tower of Babel,

00:09:08.580 --> 00:09:09.793
it was prosperity.

00:09:11.670 --> 00:09:16.200
I realized that the billionaires'
children don't work.

00:09:16.200 --> 00:09:19.080
And when the boss stops
being the assistant

00:09:19.080 --> 00:09:20.920
and becomes the boss,

00:09:20.920 --> 00:09:24.600
he forgets how to use
the Xerox machine, right?

00:09:24.600 --> 00:09:27.700
And he doesn't put his gas in his car.

00:09:27.700 --> 00:09:29.540
And he doesn't mow his own lawn.

00:09:29.540 --> 00:09:31.850
And he's not connected
to anything around him.

00:09:31.850 --> 00:09:34.520
So what happens to this rich person?

00:09:34.520 --> 00:09:36.470
They become frightened.

00:09:36.470 --> 00:09:39.100
They surround themselves with security.

00:09:39.100 --> 00:09:42.120
And they know themselves
to be a flawed human being,

00:09:42.120 --> 00:09:43.447
but they forget, they say, "Wait a second.

00:09:43.447 --> 00:09:46.487
"I'm like a God, there's
nothing that I can't buy.

00:09:46.487 --> 00:09:47.537
"There's nothing that I can't do.

00:09:47.537 --> 00:09:49.607
"I'm the richest country in the world.

00:09:49.607 --> 00:09:52.007
"I don't have to work.

00:09:52.007 --> 00:09:55.627
"Oh, Americans won't do stoop labor,

00:09:55.627 --> 00:09:57.627
"we'll open the borders," right?

00:09:57.627 --> 00:10:00.437
"I don't have to defend myself, ha-ha,

00:10:00.437 --> 00:10:03.270
"because we all really
want the same things."

00:10:03.270 --> 00:10:05.097
So as soon as you say,
"It's geopolitical,"

00:10:05.097 --> 00:10:06.910
"we all really want the same things,"

00:10:06.910 --> 00:10:08.837
some other guy over there is gonna say,

00:10:08.837 --> 00:10:11.400
"These guys are suckers, I'm
gonna take advantage of 'em,"

00:10:11.400 --> 00:10:13.080
that's the history of the world.

00:10:13.080 --> 00:10:14.397
That's what we see now.

00:10:15.850 --> 00:10:17.990
- So with all of that in mind, I mean,

00:10:17.990 --> 00:10:19.850
that's very much what you're
talking about in the book,

00:10:19.850 --> 00:10:24.850
so do you think there is a
rebirth of people realizing

00:10:24.860 --> 00:10:29.860
some of this related to either
the Bible or age-old stories

00:10:30.460 --> 00:10:33.650
versus sort of the purely
secularism on steroids

00:10:33.650 --> 00:10:36.240
that we seem to be stuck in at the moment?

00:10:36.240 --> 00:10:37.620
- Well, I don't think it's a rebirth.

00:10:37.620 --> 00:10:38.740
I think it's there all the time.

00:10:38.740 --> 00:10:42.530
Half of the country anyway,
voted for Donald Trump.

00:10:42.530 --> 00:10:44.540
These people, and you know who it is?

00:10:44.540 --> 00:10:47.630
It's what happens when the
people become too prosperous,

00:10:47.630 --> 00:10:50.840
is the country divides, any country,

00:10:50.840 --> 00:10:53.650
into the rich and the powerful, and then,

00:10:53.650 --> 00:10:57.020
the parasites who live off
of the rich and the powerful,

00:10:57.020 --> 00:10:58.200
wanna supplant them.

00:10:58.200 --> 00:11:01.170
For example, Black Lives
Matter, you know, and the Squad,

00:11:01.170 --> 00:11:05.070
and the poor who are
getting bribed to stay poor,

00:11:05.070 --> 00:11:07.120
which is what we see in the other cities.

00:11:08.490 --> 00:11:13.490
But the people who have
always been America

00:11:13.500 --> 00:11:15.880
have been the middle class, right?

00:11:15.880 --> 00:11:18.880
Been the doctor, the lawyer,
the butcher, the baker,

00:11:18.880 --> 00:11:20.850
the candlestick maker, the shoemaker,

00:11:20.850 --> 00:11:22.510
because they have to deal with each other.

00:11:22.510 --> 00:11:24.460
They have to deal with the supply system.

00:11:24.460 --> 00:11:25.700
They have to deal with customers.

00:11:25.700 --> 00:11:27.610
They have to deal with credit.

00:11:27.610 --> 00:11:29.710
The rich don't have to deal
with any of those things,

00:11:29.710 --> 00:11:30.930
and neither do the poor.

00:11:30.930 --> 00:11:34.130
So the middle class is very,
very connected to each other,

00:11:34.130 --> 00:11:37.500
but the middle class is
vanishing, vanishing, vanishing.

00:11:37.500 --> 00:11:38.997
And especially with the,

00:11:39.835 --> 00:11:42.400
with A, with the
centralization of distribution,

00:11:42.400 --> 00:11:47.220
and then, with outsourcing
everything to China.

00:11:47.220 --> 00:11:49.640
So, but there's still a middle class,

00:11:49.640 --> 00:11:52.120
and it's basically in the South, right?

00:11:52.120 --> 00:11:55.000
Because the South is the last vestiges

00:11:55.000 --> 00:11:58.363
of the wonderful heritage
of agrarianism, right?

00:11:59.597 --> 00:12:00.827
And the farmer is, Harry Truman, said,

00:12:00.827 --> 00:12:02.700
"The farmer is the
smartest guy in the world,"

00:12:02.700 --> 00:12:07.230
'cause he's sitting on his
tractor 12 hours a day.

00:12:07.230 --> 00:12:10.380
And he's thinking about the weather,

00:12:10.380 --> 00:12:13.000
and about the railroads,
and about the credit,

00:12:13.000 --> 00:12:16.290
and about the price of grain, and about...

00:12:16.290 --> 00:12:20.600
The farmer realizes that every
moment he spends on task A,

00:12:22.070 --> 00:12:25.480
he can't spend on task B, right?

00:12:25.480 --> 00:12:29.140
Which is Tom Sowell, right?

00:12:29.140 --> 00:12:32.520
And which is Hayek, and
which is Milton Friedman.

00:12:32.520 --> 00:12:35.090
It goes back to, you
know, John Stuart, no.

