00:36:21.347 --> 00:36:24.067
"come to some mutual
agreement," so that's the right.

00:36:24.067 --> 00:36:27.580
So that's why the right
gets accused of everything.

00:36:27.580 --> 00:36:30.110
- So how do you think
we shift that paradigm?

00:36:30.110 --> 00:36:32.717
I'm sure you've heard the phrase,
the Breitbart phrase that,

00:36:32.717 --> 00:36:34.660
"politics is downstream from culture."

00:36:34.660 --> 00:36:37.250
The right obviously lost culture

00:36:37.250 --> 00:36:39.000
over a certain amount of decades.

00:36:39.000 --> 00:36:41.760
I think that actually
is shifting right now,

00:36:41.760 --> 00:36:42.730
a little bit at least,

00:36:42.730 --> 00:36:44.530
but in terms of art and everything else,

00:36:44.530 --> 00:36:46.340
is there something better that,

00:36:46.340 --> 00:36:48.490
say right-leaning or conservative?

00:36:48.490 --> 00:36:49.730
I mean, you described yourself before

00:36:49.730 --> 00:36:50.563
as an old-school liberal.

00:36:50.563 --> 00:36:52.630
I mean, that's what put me on the map.

00:36:52.630 --> 00:36:54.750
I was trying to save
liberalism from the left

00:36:54.750 --> 00:36:57.370
five years ago, or seven years ago.

00:36:57.370 --> 00:36:59.960
But for any of us that are
roughly freedom-loving,

00:36:59.960 --> 00:37:02.640
you just want people to live
and let live kind of people,

00:37:02.640 --> 00:37:05.523
what can we do culturally or artistically,

00:37:06.683 --> 00:37:08.260
or through the type of work that you do,

00:37:08.260 --> 00:37:09.630
to turn this thing around?

00:37:09.630 --> 00:37:11.470
'Cause it's not gonna
be the political leaders

00:37:11.470 --> 00:37:12.760
that turn it around, obviously.

00:37:12.760 --> 00:37:14.310
- Yeah, we can't do anything,

00:37:14.310 --> 00:37:16.750
'cause that's not the purpose of art.

00:37:16.750 --> 00:37:21.270
Art is the conjunction of inspiration

00:37:21.270 --> 00:37:23.550
with an audience,

00:37:23.550 --> 00:37:25.460
that the artist has some inspiration.

00:37:25.460 --> 00:37:27.460
And that the inspiration is reducible

00:37:27.460 --> 00:37:30.614
to be kind to Black people,
or hate white people,

00:37:30.614 --> 00:37:35.614
or people dying of cancer
deserve our compassion.

00:37:35.740 --> 00:37:37.150
That's not inspiration.

00:37:37.150 --> 00:37:39.044
So that's what art is.

00:37:39.044 --> 00:37:40.240
Art is

00:37:40.240 --> 00:37:43.000
very closely akin to religion.

00:37:43.000 --> 00:37:47.730
And it's not at all connected
to politics or culture.

00:37:47.730 --> 00:37:50.830
Art, it's like the oyster, right?

00:37:50.830 --> 00:37:51.910
The oyster makes the pearl,

00:37:51.910 --> 00:37:54.420
but the oyster can't use the pearl.

00:37:54.420 --> 00:37:57.620
But other people can use the
pearl, but the oyster can't.

00:37:57.620 --> 00:37:59.720
The oyster isn't making that pearl

00:37:59.720 --> 00:38:02.800
to influence people to
dress up their honey

00:38:02.800 --> 00:38:05.400
when they go out to the nightclub, right?

00:38:05.400 --> 00:38:08.660
The oyster has to make the
pearl or it's gonna go nuts.

00:38:08.660 --> 00:38:10.100
There's nothing that art can do.

00:38:10.100 --> 00:38:14.030
What we can do is speak
the truth to each other,

00:38:14.030 --> 00:38:16.503
and not give in.

00:38:16.503 --> 00:38:20.990
And not because it's the
daily erosion of life.

00:38:21.990 --> 00:38:24.473
I'm gonna tell you my five pronouns,

00:38:24.473 --> 00:38:28.990
or wait a second, you
have to identify yourself,

00:38:28.990 --> 00:38:30.560
or are you vaccinated,

00:38:30.560 --> 00:38:33.420
this constant shepherding
each other day-to-day,

00:38:33.420 --> 00:38:35.700
which is killing.

00:38:35.700 --> 00:38:40.610
And to be able to deal with
this retail is exhausting.

00:38:40.610 --> 00:38:42.247
It has to be dealt with wholesale.

00:38:42.247 --> 00:38:44.923
And the way the individual
deals with wholesale,

00:38:44.923 --> 00:38:48.357
say, "Thanks, I'm not playing,
I'm not playing that game."

00:38:49.520 --> 00:38:51.490
You know, it's like what
we learned from, you know,

00:38:51.490 --> 00:38:54.370
I learn it anyway, from gay
friends, gay liberation,

00:38:54.370 --> 00:38:56.277
30 years ago, 40 years ago,

00:38:56.277 --> 00:39:00.337
"We're here, we're queer,
get used to it," right?

00:39:00.337 --> 00:39:03.096
"It's not my problem that I'm gay," right?

00:39:03.096 --> 00:39:05.690
"If you've got a problem with
it, that's your problem."

00:39:05.690 --> 00:39:07.997
So that's what we have to do,
we have to speak up and say,

00:39:07.997 --> 00:39:11.490
"And in fact, we're here,
we're queer, get used to it,"

00:39:11.490 --> 00:39:14.093
because then it starts
to crumble a little bit.

00:39:15.400 --> 00:39:18.540
When that look on people's
face, you know, you've seen it,

00:39:18.540 --> 00:39:21.757
where they say, "Oh my god,
he's not playing my game."

00:39:23.400 --> 00:39:26.100
- That actually gets nicely to a phrase

00:39:26.100 --> 00:39:27.970
that's in the book that I really loved.

00:39:27.970 --> 00:39:31.360
You talk about a "virus of conformity."

00:39:31.360 --> 00:39:36.025
And that seems to be it almost
more than anything else,

00:39:36.025 --> 00:39:39.770
that the media, the
politicians, the people,

00:39:39.770 --> 00:39:44.770
you know, COVID vaxes, gender
pronouns, the whole thing,

00:39:44.850 --> 00:39:47.910
that everyone is in on a bunch of stuff

00:39:47.910 --> 00:39:49.723
that nobody really believes.

00:39:51.280 --> 00:39:52.840
- That's true.

00:39:52.840 --> 00:39:56.023
But as Shakespeare, another Jew, said,

00:39:58.287 --> 00:40:01.787
"Truth's a bitch that
must be whipped to kennel,

00:40:01.787 --> 00:40:05.120
"while Lady the Brach can
sit by the fire and stink."

00:40:05.120 --> 00:40:07.933
Lady the Brach means a female dog.

00:40:09.170 --> 00:40:12.253
Truth's always a bitch that
must be whipped to kennel.

00:40:13.350 --> 00:40:15.670
Okay, here's the thing about religion,

00:40:15.670 --> 00:40:17.167
a thing about the Torah is it says,

00:40:17.167 --> 00:40:19.367
"Behave in this way and you'll be,

00:40:19.367 --> 00:40:20.617
"that's what God wants.

00:40:20.617 --> 00:40:22.437
"He wants you to do justice, love mercy,

00:40:22.437 --> 00:40:23.970
"and walk humbly with God."

00:40:23.970 --> 00:40:28.630
The rest of it is not
in your control, right?

00:40:28.630 --> 00:40:32.040
Like they say, like it was
Cicero or somebody who said,

00:40:32.040 --> 00:40:33.107
when someone said,

00:40:33.107 --> 00:40:34.783
"What do you do when the tyrant says,

00:40:34.783 --> 00:40:37.100
"'Do A, B, and C, or I'll kill you?'"

00:40:38.010 --> 00:40:39.277
He said, "You say to the tyrant,

00:40:39.277 --> 00:40:41.370
"'I never told you I was immortal.'"

00:40:43.010 --> 00:40:46.120
So the question is, if
you were given the chance,

00:40:46.120 --> 00:40:47.347
if they came to you and they said,

00:40:47.347 --> 00:40:49.227
"Okay, here's what I want you to do.

00:40:49.227 --> 00:40:51.337
"I need you to recant," right?

00:40:51.337 --> 00:40:54.297
"Because, A, if you don't
recant, we're gonna ruin you.

00:40:54.297 --> 00:40:57.247
"And if you do recant, we're
gonna make you a billionaire."

00:40:59.590 --> 00:41:01.690
Up to you, what are you gonna say?

00:41:01.690 --> 00:41:02.950
- Well, A, I would know they're

00:41:02.950 --> 00:41:05.070
most likely gonna make
you a slave, actually,

00:41:05.070 --> 00:41:06.920
not a billionaire if you do recant,

00:41:06.920 --> 00:41:10.130
especially if you're recanting
for something you didn't do.

00:41:10.130 --> 00:41:12.650
- Exactly, so, but another
way to look at it is,

00:41:12.650 --> 00:41:14.960
I never told you I was immortal.

00:41:14.960 --> 00:41:15.793
- Yeah.
- Right?

00:41:15.793 --> 00:41:19.860
Are you gonna say that
you're going to ruin me

00:41:19.860 --> 00:41:22.070
for not proclaiming that men and women

00:41:22.070 --> 00:41:23.770
can turn into each other?

00:41:23.770 --> 00:41:26.970
Well, that's up to you, it's not up to me.

00:41:26.970 --> 00:41:30.510
What's up to me is my conscience, right?

00:41:30.510 --> 00:41:33.620
Now, am I gonna fall off the
wagon and be a coward sometime,

00:41:33.620 --> 00:41:36.090
be a fool sometime, and be wishy-washy?

00:41:36.090 --> 00:41:38.160
Probably, so what do you do then?

00:41:38.160 --> 00:41:39.930
You get back on the wagon.

00:41:39.930 --> 00:41:43.480
And you meet the best people
in the world, don't ya?

00:41:43.480 --> 00:41:44.520
- You absolutely do.
- Yeah.

00:41:44.520 --> 00:41:46.920
- The people that I've met on
the other side of this thing,

00:41:46.920 --> 00:41:48.670
it's incomparable
- I know.

00:41:48.670 --> 00:41:50.250
- to what I was doing before.

00:41:50.250 --> 00:41:51.600
- I know.
- Incomparable.

00:41:51.600 --> 00:41:53.780
- It's like Ruth Wisse at
Harvard, teaching at Harvard.

00:41:53.780 --> 00:41:55.867
And teacher comes up to her and says,

00:41:55.867 --> 00:41:57.997
"Ruth, you know, I wanna
say, blah, blah, blah.

00:41:57.997 --> 00:42:00.560
"But what will happen
if I tell the truth?"

00:42:00.560 --> 00:42:02.087
Ruth said, "You'll be free."

00:42:05.134 --> 00:42:08.220
So it's better to be free,
it's exhilarating to be free.

00:42:08.220 --> 00:42:11.510
And you might as well blow
your fuckin' brains out,

00:42:11.510 --> 00:42:15.020
if having seen a little bit of the truth

00:42:15.020 --> 00:42:17.070
of our contemporary civilization,

00:42:17.070 --> 00:42:20.332
you say, "Yes, but I'm going to conform."

00:42:20.332 --> 00:42:23.960
'Cause you're dead then, you're just dead.

00:42:23.960 --> 00:42:26.093
- I got one more for ya.
- Yeah.

00:42:26.093 --> 00:42:28.370
- What's inspiring you these days?

00:42:28.370 --> 00:42:30.430
What do you look at, or see, or hear,

00:42:30.430 --> 00:42:33.163
or listen to, or read that you go,

00:42:34.077 --> 00:42:36.690
"That's got the thing
that I'm looking for,"

00:42:36.690 --> 00:42:38.900
or at least got the spirit of it, maybe?

00:42:38.900 --> 00:42:41.500
- I read everything 'cause
that's what I do all day.

00:42:42.680 --> 00:42:44.440
Years ago, I was going out to my office.

00:42:44.440 --> 00:42:46.720
My oldest daughter was about 10.

00:42:46.720 --> 00:42:47.553
She said, "Dad, where you going?"

00:42:47.553 --> 00:42:48.590
I said, "I'm going to work."

00:42:48.590 --> 00:42:51.477
She said, "Nighty-night,"
'cause that's what I do.

00:42:51.477 --> 00:42:53.490
You know, I nap all day and I read,

00:42:53.490 --> 00:42:55.900
and every once in a while,
I'll jot something down.

00:42:55.900 --> 00:42:58.570
But I discovered some really
great writers in the 20s

00:42:58.570 --> 00:42:59.423
who were writing about business.

00:42:59.423 --> 00:43:02.430
There's a guy called Jesse Sprague.

00:43:02.430 --> 00:43:04.840
And he wrote several
books about, (chuckles)

00:43:04.840 --> 00:43:09.320
about the building trades,
and about the wholesalers,

00:43:09.320 --> 00:43:11.253
and about credit, and the romances,

00:43:13.550 --> 00:43:15.210
romance, what do you call it?

00:43:15.210 --> 00:43:19.370
Fictional romances of an
imaginary guy in these trades.

00:43:19.370 --> 00:43:21.490
And he takes all of America apart.

00:43:21.490 --> 00:43:24.220
And he makes it just fascinating.

00:43:24.220 --> 00:43:27.587
The view of the 20s, check
it out, Jesse Sprague,

00:43:27.587 --> 00:43:29.910
"The Romance of Credit,"

00:43:29.910 --> 00:43:32.690
or "Confessions of a
Building Trades Contractor,"

00:43:32.690 --> 00:43:33.713
they're marvelous.

00:43:35.070 --> 00:43:36.583
- So I ask you what's inspiring you

00:43:36.583 --> 00:43:38.510
and you throw me back 100 years.

00:43:38.510 --> 00:43:40.460
That sort of says everything I suppose.

00:43:41.360 --> 00:43:44.210
- Well, there's, listen,
Bari Weiss is inspiring me.

00:43:44.210 --> 00:43:47.690
You know, Bari started at
University of Austin, Texas.

00:43:47.690 --> 00:43:49.970
And she asked me, would I play along.

00:43:49.970 --> 00:43:52.220
So I'm gonna play along,
so that's inspiring.

00:43:55.370 --> 00:43:58.057
Young people today, like Bari said to me,

00:43:58.057 --> 00:44:01.990
"Dave, Dave," she said,
"Don't be a curmudgeon."

00:44:01.990 --> 00:44:03.847
So I said, "Oh my god, that's true.

00:44:03.847 --> 00:44:06.510
"I gotta stop being a curmudgeon," right?

00:44:06.510 --> 00:44:08.117
And saying, "Oh, you know, Grandpa says,

00:44:08.117 --> 00:44:10.140
"'When I was young fall, fall, fall.'"

00:44:10.140 --> 00:44:11.490
Well, of course that's true.

00:44:11.490 --> 00:44:13.940
'Cause the point is not when I was young,

00:44:13.940 --> 00:44:14.773
things were better.

00:44:14.773 --> 00:44:17.050
Well, of course they were
better 'cause you were young.

00:44:17.050 --> 00:44:22.050
But now what? You know,
so what can I contribute?

00:44:22.340 --> 00:44:24.390
I'd love to contribute, you know?

00:44:24.390 --> 00:44:29.390
What can I contribute to my
magnificent country right now?

00:44:29.820 --> 00:44:32.817
Tell the truth the best I can, you know?

00:44:32.817 --> 00:44:33.703
And that's it.

00:44:35.090 --> 00:44:36.740
- You're speaking my language, man.

00:44:36.740 --> 00:44:38.270
David, I've really enjoyed talking to you.

00:44:38.270 --> 00:44:39.980
We're gonna link to the book down below.

00:44:39.980 --> 00:44:42.440
And I hope you'll come to
the free state of Florida.

00:44:42.440 --> 00:44:44.220
I don't know that they're
gonna let me back in LA.

00:44:44.220 --> 00:44:45.870
So I don't know that we
could break bread there,

00:44:45.870 --> 00:44:48.360
but you're welcome here
in Florida anytime.

00:44:48.360 --> 00:44:50.660
- Thank you so much, it's
a pleasure talking to you.

00:44:50.660 --> 00:44:52.330
- If you're looking for
more honest and thoughtful

